{"kind":"request","text":"Seventh-round review of the deployment note at exact head fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3; artifact c86c39b2b526a4419d39a5fdb2a0092b41f98829 records what changed against each of the five findings.\n\nTwo of the four blockers were things I asserted without measuring, and you were right about both, so the honest question for this round is what I have asserted without measuring now. Three candidates. The batch note in refs/notes/gitseq-batches is claimed to be writable in the same atomic push as a branch create and a branch delete — I have not tested whether a receiver accepts a notes-ref update in the same atomic transaction as branch updates, and if it does not, the record I am relying on to survive claim loss is written in a second push and the window reopens. The absent-value lease on a fixed claim ref is claimed to make exclusion structural, which depends on the receiver evaluating that lease atomically against concurrent creates rather than merely rejecting a stale value. And the refs/seq lease during claim acquisition binds the submission repository only if the receiver enforces leases at all, which is the same configuration-dependent assumption that was wrong twice already.\n\nI did verify the two reachability claims by execution rather than argument, in both directions: a gitlink in a tree is not reached by rev-list, and E plus every candidate as parents are reached and survive gc --prune=now with only the claim ref present. Those two I stand behind.\n\nThe remaining question is whether the state table now closes. It has four live-claim rows, a quiescent row, an anomaly catch-all, and a second no-claim check over batch notes. If there is a tuple that reaches none of them, or one that reaches the anomaly branch when it should be ordinary, that is the finding — a catch-all is only a safety property if every unlisted state actually arrives at it.","body":{"conditions":"Review notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md at exact head fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3. Judge each of the five previously-open findings closed or still open: parent-based object retention for the claim; the singleton per-genesis claim ref with absent-value lease and its structural exclusion; the retained batch note closing claim loss at P/P; recovery republishing the recorded E rather than rebuilding it; and the start precondition with its refs/seq lease. Attack the three asserted-not-measured claims named in the request, especially whether a notes-ref update can participate in the same atomic push as branch create and delete. Test whether the state table closes: any tuple reaching no row, or reaching the anomaly branch when it should be ordinary. Re-audit ALL spike cases for the cannot-fail defect class. Report APPROVED or CHANGES-REQUESTED against that exact head with verbatim full event ids.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}